          %p The idea is that a coalgebra modality allows us to perform various operations on smooth morphisms.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                If we start with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:!A \rightarrow B
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mo !
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:!A \rightarrow C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \rightarrow B \otimes C
                defined as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo ;
                        %mi f
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta;f \otimes g
            %li
              %p
                If we start with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A \rightarrow B
                which is a linear morphism, we can say that it is a very simple form of smooth morphism and obtain a morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \rightarrow B
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                defined as
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ;
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u;f
            %li
              %p
                If we start with a constant
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi I
                        %mo →
                        %mo !
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:I \rightarrow !A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \rightarrow B
                from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                defined as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo ;
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon;f
            %li
              %p
                If we start with two smooth morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:!A \rightarrow B
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo :
                        %mo !
                        %mi B
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:!B \rightarrow C
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo !
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} !A \rightarrow C
                defined as
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo ;
                        %mo !
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ;
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m;!(f);g
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A CMon-enriched
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              such that each hom-set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}[A,B]
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
              (in Set) and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \otimes -
              as well as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ;
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -;-
              are bilinear ie. preserve sums and the zero in each variable.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              A differential category is a CMon-enriched
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              together with a coalgebra modality and a deriving transformation ie. a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mo !
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d:!A \otimes A \rightarrow !A
              such that the following diagrams commute: …
          %h3#examples Examples
          %h2#codifferential_categories Codifferential categories
          %p
            The definition of a
            %em codifferential category
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"} formal dual
            of that of a differential category:
          %p
            A
            %em codifferential category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            such that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}^{op}
            is a differential category in the sense
            %a{:href => "#DifferentialCategories"} above
            (and vice versa).
          %p
            The notion of codifferential categories is maybe more intuitively accessible because it involves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            instead of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonoids"} comonoids
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonads"} comonads
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+duality"} formally dual
            notion of differential categories is closer to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+linear+logic"} differential linear logic
            with their comonadic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
          %p
            In the following sequence of definitions, the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(A)
            is to be interpreted as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              An
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+modality"} algebra modality
              in a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C}, \otimes, I)
              is given by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (S,m,u)
              and two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo :
                    %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta:I \rightarrow S(A)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
